    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=1$, $b=-1$, $c=2$.\n\nUse the first two transitions to determine the coefficient ratios, then verify $f\\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)=\\frac{n+1}{n+2}$.",
      "worked": "The first two transitions give\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\frac23&=\\frac{a}{\\frac12b+c}\\\\\nb+2c&=3a\\\\\n\\frac34&=\\frac{a}{\\frac23b+c}\\\\\n2b+3c&=4a.\n\\end{align*}$$\nDouble the first linear equation and subtract the second:\n$$\\begin{align*}\n2b+4c&=6a\\\\\nc&=2a\\\\\nb&=3a-2c\\\\\nb&=-a.\n\\end{align*}$$\nChoose $a=1$, giving $b=-1$ and $c=2$, so $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2-x}$.\n\nFor every positive integer $n$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\nf\\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{2-\\frac{n}{n+1}}\\\\\nf\\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)&=\\frac{n+1}{2(n+1)-n}\\\\\nf\\left(\\frac{n}{n+1}\\right)&=\\frac{n+1}{n+2}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThus the rule produces each subsequent term.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
